### Round
  brief:
    |A round shape.

### Square
  brief:
    |A square shape.

### einsums::fixture::Shape
  brief:
    |An enum carries doc text too, and it is rendered as reST like any other.
  detail:
    |- A bullet in an enum's detail, wrapping onto a second source line to exercise the same join.
    |- A second bullet.

### einsums::fixture::accumulate
  brief:
    |Field descriptions carrying their own markup.
  returns:
    |The accumulated result, described across two source lines so the wrap is exercised here too.
  param values:
    |The input values. Long enough to wrap onto a second source line, which reST field bodies also require be joined.
  param scale:
    |A scale factor. See ``einsums::fixture::wrapped_bullets`` for the reference form.
  tparam T:
    |The element type, as in ``std::complex<double>``.
  throw std::invalid_argument:
    |When ``values`` is empty.

### einsums::fixture::admonitions_with_lists
  brief:
    |Admonition bodies that contain lists.
  detail:
    |.. note::
    |
    |   - A bullet inside a note, whose continuation wraps onto a second source line and must stay joined.
    |   - A second bullet.
    |
    |.. warning::
    |
    |   A one-line warning.
    |
    |.. seealso::
    |
    |   einsums::fixture::wrapped_bullets

### einsums::fixture::bullets_around_code
  brief:
    |A bullet list directly adjacent to a code block.
  detail:
    |- An item before the code.
    |- A second item.
    |
    |.. code-block:: py
    |
    |   result = wrapped_bullets()
    |
    |- An item after the code, opening a second list.

### einsums::fixture::definition_list
  brief:
    |A definition list.
  detail:
    |KNOWN BAD (Finding 4, same root cause as nested_bullets): a definition list
    |IS its indentation, so ltrimming every line flattens it into one paragraph.
    |
    |term one
    |The definition of the first term, which wraps onto a second line.
    |term two
    |The definition of the second term.

### einsums::fixture::embedded_rst
  brief:
    |Verbatim reST spliced through an rst span.
  detail:
    |KNOWN BAD (Finding 5): naming the span command in the brief above - writing
    |it inside an inline literal, as prose about it has to - opens a real span.
    |Span protection scans the raw text and does not know that a backtick-quoted
    |command is not a command, so the opener inside the literal pairs with the
    |genuine terminator below and swallows the whole comment.
    |
    |.. note::
    |
    |An authored directive, indented body and all.
    |
    |- A list inside the spliced block.
    |- A second item.

### einsums::fixture::enumerated_list
  brief:
    |An enumerated list whose items wrap.
  detail:
    |1. The first step, written long enough that it wraps onto a second source line the way an author would naturally write it.
    |2. The second step.
    |3. The third step.

### einsums::fixture::fussy_inline_spans
  brief:
    |Literal and emphasis spans that reST is fussy about.
  detail:
    |A template argument list after a literal, ``std::vector<int>``, and a
    |subscript, ``extents[k]``, and a call, *compute()*, each of which must be
    |absorbed into the span so the closing delimiter is not followed by a
    |character reST reads as a dangling start-string.

### einsums::fixture::inline_command_at_line_start
  brief:
    |An inline command at the start of a continuation line.
  detail:
    |An author wrapping a sentence has no control over which token lands first on
    |a line. A line whose first token starts with a command marker used to be
    |taken for a block-level command, so the line below neither joined its bullet
    |nor passed through inline conversion: the item dedented to column 0 (which is
    |what Sphinx reported, about a generated file) and the reference lost its
    |markup. It must stay one joined item with a literal-formatted reference.
    |
    |- A bullet whose continuation line begins with an inline command, such as ``einsums::fixture::wrapped_bullets``, and then continues with prose that should stay joined to the same item.
    |- A second bullet, to close the list.

### einsums::fixture::inline_commands_wrapped
  brief:
    |Every inline command, each opening a continuation line.
  detail:
    |The rest of the closed set, in a plain paragraph rather than a list. Nothing
    |about the reST is invalid here, so the markup check cannot see it: the text
    |golden is the only thing that catches an inline command silently losing its
    |markup.
    |
    |A paragraph that wraps so that
    |``std::complex<double>`` starts a line, and another that wraps so that
    |``count`` starts one, and
    |*emphasis*, and
    |**bold**, and
    |*also_emphasis* all do too.

### einsums::fixture::math_next_to_list
  brief:
    |Math spans inline and as a block, next to a list.
  detail:
    |The inline form :math:`a_{ij} b_{jk}` sits in running prose, and the block
    |form stands alone:
    |
    |.. math::
    |
    |   C_{ik} = \sum_j a_{ij} b_{jk}
    |
    |- A list immediately after the math block.
    |- A second item.

### einsums::fixture::nested_bullets
  brief:
    |A nested bullet list.
  detail:
    |KNOWN BAD (Finding 4): the converter is line-oriented and ltrims every line,
    |so the indentation that expresses nesting is gone before anything looks at
    |it. The result is valid reST and a flat four-item list - which is why only
    |the text golden catches this one, not the markup check.
    |
    |- An outer item.
    |- An inner item, indented under it.
    |- A second inner item.
    |- A second outer item.

### einsums::fixture::par_with_bullets
  brief:
    |A ``@par`` block whose body is a bullet list.
  detail:
    |**Limitations**
    |
    |- A directive body is accumulated separately and joined by its own helper, so it is a second code path with the same wrapped-continuation problem.
    |- A second item, to close the list.

### einsums::fixture::wrapped_bullets
  brief:
    |A bullet list whose items wrap across source lines.
  detail:
    |The wrapped continuation lines must be joined onto their item. reST requires
    |a bullet item's continuation to be indented to the item's text column, and
    |the comment normalizer has already stripped that indentation, so anything
    |other than joining produces "Bullet list ends without a blank line".
    |
    |- The first item is long enough that it wraps onto a second source line and then onto a third, all of which belong to the same item.
    |- The second item, to prove the list continues.
    |
    |A trailing paragraph, which must be separated from the list by a blank line.

